<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Vatican City</h1><p class="st-meta">1929 CE – present · capital Vatican City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The world's smallest sovereign state, an enclave in Rome created in 1929 to guarantee the independence of the pope and the Holy See.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a> in 1870, the popes refused to recognise the loss and considered themselves prisoners in the Vatican. The Roman Question was settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 11 February 1929, signed by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-gasparri">Pietro Gasparri</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>, creating Vatican City.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state covers about 44 hectares around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">Saint Peter's Basilica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apostolic-palace">Apostolic Palace</a>. The pope is an absolute elective monarch, elected by cardinals in conclave, and governs through a commission and a governor; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontifical-swiss-guard">Swiss Guard</a> protects him. Vatican City supports the worldwide government of the Holy See, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-radio">Vatican Radio</a> founded in 1931, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-museums">Vatican Museums</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Apostolic Library</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-vatican-council">Second Vatican Council</a> of 1962–1965 transformed the Church's relations with the modern world. The concordat with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was revised in 1984. Vatican City continues today; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiv">Pope Leo XIV</a> was elected in 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vatican-city.jpg" alt="Vatican City" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Goran_tek-en · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vatican_City_(2023%E2%80%93present).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1929 CE – present</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vatican City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pius XI, John Paul II, Leo XIV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Vatican City</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vatican City State, Holy See</dd></dl></aside></div>
